      "text": "To find the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to set it to zero:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo make it easier to solve, we can multiply the entire equation by \\(-1\\):\n\n\\[\nt^2 - 12t + 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula, which is given by:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\n\nFor our equation \\( t^2 - 12t + 27 = 0 \\), the coefficients are \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\). Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nThe solutions to the equation are \\( t = 9 \\) and \\( t = 3 \\). The largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
