      "text": "To find the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we start by setting the temperature function equal to 77. The temperature function is given by:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77 \\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange this equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0 \\]\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNext, we multiply the entire equation by -1 to make the coefficient of \\( t^2 \\) positive:\n\n\\[ t^2 - 12t + 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula, which is given by:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\]\n\nHere, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\). Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2} \\]\n\nThis gives us two potential solutions for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9 \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\]\n\nThe largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is \\( t = 9 \\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]",
